        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>The evolutionary appearance of lignin in higher plants coincided with development of a terrestrial vertical growth habit, and lignin is generally considered to be the critical structural polymer needed for upright growth against gravity. In view of this evolutionary coincidence, it has been postulated that lignin deposition is mediated by gravity. The purpose of this investigation was to determine the effects of microgravity on the production of lignin in higher plants.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>Two miniature greenhouses, called plant growth units (PGUs), containing four and ten-day-old pine seedlings, mung bean seeds, and oat seeds were flown as part of the Spacelab-2 payload. The growth units were sealed and the atmosphere of each chamber was exchanged with gas mixtures containing known amounts of oxygen and carbon dioxide. During flight, temperature and lamp status data were monitored from a control panel. Three times a day, crew members checked the temperatures inside the growth chambers; twice a day, gas samples and photographs were taken. After landing, the seedlings were photographed and measured, and subsequently they were sectioned and analyzed for lignin content and related enzyme activities. An identical group of plants were later grown as ground controls, utilizing the temperatures and gas measurements obtained inflight.<P><B>Results:</B><P>The lignin content of flight seedlings was significantly reduced in all three plant species in comparison to controls. In the youngest pine seedlings (four days old at launch, twelve days old at harvest), lignin content of flight seedlings averaged 13 percent less than that of controls. In the oldest pine seedlings (ten days old at launch, eighteen days old at harvest), lignin content of flight seedlings was 8 percent less than that of controls. Lignin content in flight mung beans and oats averaged 24 percent and 23 percent less than lignin in controls. In all cases, the difference in lignin content was statistically significant at least at the 0.01 percent level. This reduction in lignin in all three species grown in microgravity provides direct evidence that gravity is an important factor in lignification.
        </div>

